WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.873
(audience cheering)

00:00:00.873 --> 00:00:02.910
- Oh my god.
- It's crazy.

00:00:02.910 --> 00:00:04.110
- I was like, I had no idea.

00:00:04.110 --> 00:00:05.370
I've never met you in person.

00:00:05.370 --> 00:00:06.630
I was like, "You are tall."

00:00:06.630 --> 00:00:07.587
- Can I just start by saying,

00:00:07.587 --> 00:00:09.450
I'm fanning out a little bit.

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:11.010
- Oh my gosh, I am too.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:12.336
Of you and your height.

00:00:12.336 --> 00:00:13.890
(everyone laughing)

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:16.380
- You're such a big, big hit in my house.

00:00:16.380 --> 00:00:17.940
My wife and daughter in particular,

00:00:17.940 --> 00:00:19.110
are huge fans of yours, so.

00:00:19.110 --> 00:00:20.680
- They have great taste.
- They do.

00:00:20.680 --> 00:00:21.638
(audience applauding)

00:00:21.638 --> 00:00:22.732
Of course they, well-
- Well, thank you.

00:00:22.732 --> 00:00:23.827
- I mean, yes.
- That's so nice.

00:00:23.827 --> 00:00:26.430
That's so nice. I'm a huge
fan, I've been so excited.

00:00:26.430 --> 00:00:28.290
I literally am gonna be talking
about this all day, though.

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:29.580
I had no idea you were that tall.

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:30.554
And I was like, "Oh my god."

00:00:30.554 --> 00:00:33.270
And I'm wearing heels, so whatever.

00:00:33.270 --> 00:00:34.206
But happy belated birthday.

00:00:34.206 --> 00:00:35.190
- Well, thank you.
- A few weeks ago,

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:36.480
you had a huge birthday.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:37.313
It's a big birthday.

00:00:37.313 --> 00:00:39.162
- You know, Kelly. I
don't count them anymore.

00:00:39.162 --> 00:00:39.995
- You don't?

00:00:39.995 --> 00:00:41.640
- No. I mean, I feel blessed

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:43.164
to have another day of life for sure, but-

00:00:43.164 --> 00:00:44.799
- That's why I do count them.

00:00:44.799 --> 00:00:45.632
Not everybody gets them.

00:00:45.632 --> 00:00:46.470
- We're not gonna talk numbers.

00:00:46.470 --> 00:00:47.400
- Oh, no, you don't have to.

00:00:47.400 --> 00:00:49.470
But I just, not everybody gets those,

00:00:49.470 --> 00:00:50.400
you know, that many years.

00:00:50.400 --> 00:00:52.230
So it is a blessing.
- It is true.

00:00:52.230 --> 00:00:53.310
- Yeah, and it's good to celebrate.

00:00:53.310 --> 00:00:55.890
I'm opposite. I don't care about age.

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:56.880
I don't really care.

00:00:56.880 --> 00:00:57.780
- Age is a funny thing.

00:00:57.780 --> 00:01:00.240
You know, what I find is that,

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:01.980
my god, as I've gotten older,

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:05.040
I just get more sensitive, like-

00:01:05.040 --> 00:01:05.873
- Oh, yeah. I think-

00:01:05.873 --> 00:01:08.010
- I'll watch your show,

00:01:08.010 --> 00:01:08.843
your other show,

00:01:08.843 --> 00:01:09.900
I'll watch you on "The Voice"

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:13.020
and I'll watch a young singer

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:15.360
who aspires to be a
superstar like you and-

00:01:15.360 --> 00:01:16.260
- [Kelly] Oh, the stories get you.

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:18.180
- And, the stories for sure,

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:20.190
but the song itself will move me to tears.

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:22.650
And my son, who's 17 and kinda tough,

00:01:22.650 --> 00:01:24.613
looks over at me and goes,

00:01:24.613 --> 00:01:25.446
"Dad, what-"
- "What are you doing?"

00:01:25.446 --> 00:01:27.710
- "Why are you crying?
Like, why are you crying?"

00:01:27.710 --> 00:01:28.543
(Kelly laughing)

00:01:28.543 --> 00:01:30.180
I said, "Buddy, I cry
at coffee commercials.

00:01:30.180 --> 00:01:32.190
Like, I don't know why I'm crying."

00:01:32.190 --> 00:01:33.030
As I've gotten older,

00:01:33.030 --> 00:01:34.290
I don't know what's going on with me,

00:01:34.290 --> 00:01:35.123
but, I don't know.

00:01:35.123 --> 00:01:36.270
Maybe it's that we're all-
- That's amazing. I feel like-

00:01:36.270 --> 00:01:37.853
- Facing mortality now.
I don't know what it is.

00:01:37.853 --> 00:01:39.090
- I don't know, but I also feel like,

00:01:39.090 --> 00:01:40.620
men, I think you've,

00:01:40.620 --> 00:01:42.720
I think you're, especially my generation,

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:43.620
your generation like,

00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:45.840
they were taught they weren't
allowed to be emotional.

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.000
You were supposed to be
strong or whatever growing up?

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:48.833
And I feel like,

00:01:48.833 --> 00:01:49.830
I don't know, over the course of time

00:01:49.830 --> 00:01:50.663
I think each (indistinct)

00:01:50.663 --> 00:01:51.855
and your just like,
whatever it's touching.

00:01:51.855 --> 00:01:53.080
I'm gonna let it out.

00:01:53.080 --> 00:01:54.453
I'm gonna cry.

00:01:55.650 --> 00:01:57.431
- Yeah, that's not the lesson
I teach in my household.

00:01:57.431 --> 00:01:58.410
(Kelly laughing)

00:01:58.410 --> 00:01:59.547
You're free and welcome
to cry at any moment.

00:01:59.547 --> 00:02:01.050
- Yeah, I know, but we're a different,

00:02:01.050 --> 00:02:02.700
I'm teaching my children
differently, as well,

00:02:02.700 --> 00:02:04.410
but I just meant from our
generation it was always like,

00:02:04.410 --> 00:02:05.243
I'm from the South.

00:02:05.243 --> 00:02:06.630
Like, it was like, don't show emotion.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:07.745
- Oh, of course.
- That is weak.

00:02:07.745 --> 00:02:08.578
- Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:02:08.578 --> 00:02:09.411
- Whatever.

00:02:09.411 --> 00:02:11.124
Well, with two kids at home,

00:02:11.124 --> 00:02:13.830
do you and your wife ever
get away by yourself?

00:02:13.830 --> 00:02:14.730
- You know, it's rare,

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:18.390
but we thankfully had an
opportunity to get away,

00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:19.223
just the two of us.

00:02:19.223 --> 00:02:21.870
Whenever we do go on vacation,
it's always with the kids.

00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:24.018
But we got an opportunity
head off to Hawaii,

00:02:24.018 --> 00:02:26.880
and it was just a couple
days after Thanksgiving,

00:02:26.880 --> 00:02:29.370
but it wasn't without a little
bit of a hiccup because,

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:30.660
you know, we flew in from the East Coast

00:02:30.660 --> 00:02:32.520
and we had a connecting flight in Phoenix

00:02:32.520 --> 00:02:34.080
before we went on to the Big Island.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.210
But as we were coming into Phoenix,

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:39.060
the flight attendant comes up
to me and my wife and says,

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:42.330
"Um, is your final destination Kona?"

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:44.460
And we said, "Yeah, it
is. We're really excited."

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:46.050
And she goes, "Have you seen this?"

00:02:46.050 --> 00:02:46.923
And she showed me-

00:02:46.923 --> 00:02:47.949
- [Kelly] The volcano, yeah.

00:02:47.949 --> 00:02:48.782
(Kelly laughing)

00:02:48.782 --> 00:02:52.320
- The CNN report that
the Mauna Loa Volcano

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:54.570
was erupting for the
first time in 40 years.

00:02:54.570 --> 00:02:55.620
- [Kelly] And you haven't been on like,

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:57.422
a date or vacation-
- No! In like eight years.

00:02:57.422 --> 00:02:58.875
- [Kelly] And you're like, "What?" Yeah.

00:02:58.875 --> 00:03:00.030
- So we immediately
called the travel agent.

00:03:00.030 --> 00:03:02.010
She said, "I haven't even heard of it,

00:03:02.010 --> 00:03:03.780
but I think it's fine. You
should should head on."

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:07.428
So we get there, and of course
we check into the hotel,

00:03:07.428 --> 00:03:10.830
and behind the hotel is a red sky.

00:03:10.830 --> 00:03:12.150
Mind you, it's 10 o'clock at night.

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:14.490
But it looked like the sun was rising.

00:03:14.490 --> 00:03:16.680
It wasn't the sun, it
was the active volcano.

00:03:16.680 --> 00:03:18.900
- Were you not nervous about landing

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:20.640
in a place where that's happening?

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:21.766
- You know, my wife said,

00:03:21.766 --> 00:03:25.020
"Let's be a little more
optimistic about it.

00:03:25.020 --> 00:03:28.350
Maybe it's God's way of
just showing us fireworks

00:03:28.350 --> 00:03:29.220
for our arrival."

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:30.900
- Oh my god, an optimist.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:33.480
You married a glass
half full. That's nice.

00:03:33.480 --> 00:03:34.380
I would've been like, mm-

00:03:34.380 --> 00:03:35.340
- She's gonna call me on that.
- Let's go to Cabo.

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:36.270
- She didn't actually say that.

00:03:36.270 --> 00:03:38.010
- Yeah.
- I thought it.

00:03:38.010 --> 00:03:40.050
- Yeah. I was like, wait, no.

00:03:40.050 --> 00:03:41.400
You and your wife just celebrated,

00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:43.050
is it the 20th anniversary?

00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:44.281
- Yes.
- That's incredible.

00:03:44.281 --> 00:03:45.150
(audience cheering)

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:48.133
I think that it's hard.

00:03:48.133 --> 00:03:51.390
It can be hard like
growing, you know, together

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:52.860
in a marriage. That's incredible.

00:03:52.860 --> 00:03:55.320
- You know, relationships,
marriages can be hard.

00:03:55.320 --> 00:03:57.431
Ours is not. You know-
- Rub it in, yeah.

00:03:57.431 --> 00:03:59.383
- Life is hard.
- Kidding.

00:03:59.383 --> 00:04:04.383
- But what should be easy is
a kind of a loving harmony

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:06.420
with your spouse.

00:04:06.420 --> 00:04:07.710
Of course, you're always
gonna have differences

00:04:07.710 --> 00:04:08.543
of opinion or whatever,

00:04:08.543 --> 00:04:11.880
but if the relationship is
based on love, mutual respect

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:13.860
and a great liking for one another,

00:04:13.860 --> 00:04:15.150
and a little bit of heat,

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:16.290
which also-
- Hey!

00:04:16.290 --> 00:04:18.033
- Always goes a long way.
- Yeah.

00:04:19.290 --> 00:04:20.790
- Then you can take on life together.

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:22.020
Life is filled with challenges

00:04:22.020 --> 00:04:25.286
and we certainly all have faced ours,

00:04:25.286 --> 00:04:27.720
but she's my best friend.

00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:28.800
- I was gonna say, it's probably-

00:04:28.800 --> 00:04:29.970
- She makes me laugh.
- She's your best friend.

00:04:29.970 --> 00:04:31.110
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:04:31.110 --> 00:04:32.670
- You know, she was a working actor

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:34.542
when we met and got together

00:04:34.542 --> 00:04:37.905
and she put all of that on
hold to raise my two children.

00:04:37.905 --> 00:04:39.870
I have a daughter, Sophia, who's 20,

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:41.576
who's a child with special needs

00:04:41.576 --> 00:04:43.800
and who will spend her life with us.

00:04:43.800 --> 00:04:47.460
And so it was a, some
might consider a big ask

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:51.180
for my wife to put her art on hold,

00:04:51.180 --> 00:04:52.800
but she doesn't see it that way at all.

00:04:52.800 --> 00:04:54.660
Like, our number one priority as parents

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:56.700
is to love and nurture our children

00:04:56.700 --> 00:05:01.700
and make sure they have an
opportunity to succeed in life.

00:05:02.070 --> 00:05:03.605
- You met a great one.

00:05:03.605 --> 00:05:04.604
(audience cheering)

00:05:04.604 --> 00:05:06.510
So the new show looks great.

00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:07.920
It's called "Poker Face."

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:09.457
So tell us what it's about.

00:05:09.457 --> 00:05:11.730
- "Poker Face" is the brainchild

00:05:11.730 --> 00:05:14.190
of Ryan Johnson and Natasha Lyonne.

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:15.880
Two dear friends who got together

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:19.230
and came up with this show

00:05:19.230 --> 00:05:22.170
that really is a kind of a
nod to the murder mystery

00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:23.283
from the seventies.

00:05:24.420 --> 00:05:26.430
The premise of the show is a young woman

00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:28.080
by the name of Charlie Cale,

00:05:28.080 --> 00:05:30.120
who has this innate
ability to be able to tell

00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:31.770
when someone is lying.

00:05:31.770 --> 00:05:33.510
But for her, that's not the fun.

00:05:33.510 --> 00:05:35.760
The fun is to discover
why a person is lying.

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:39.600
But wherever she goes, dead
people seem to show up.

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:41.504
And so her, you know,

00:05:41.504 --> 00:05:44.520
she's the kind of person
who likes to use that skill

00:05:44.520 --> 00:05:46.170
to help people who are down on their luck.

00:05:46.170 --> 00:05:48.090
So it's not really a murder mystery show

00:05:48.090 --> 00:05:50.820
as much as it it is a how to catch them.

00:05:50.820 --> 00:05:51.653
- [Kelly] Yeah.

00:05:51.653 --> 00:05:55.053
- So the pilot finds her as a
cocktail waitress at a casino.

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:57.870
We discover very early that someone dies

00:05:57.870 --> 00:05:59.100
and she goes about the rest

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:02.730
of the episode discovering
who's responsible and why.

00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:05.760
- Man, are you good at
telling if someone's lying?

00:06:05.760 --> 00:06:08.070
I'm really not.

00:06:08.070 --> 00:06:09.861
- I think I have a
pretty good BS detector.

00:06:09.861 --> 00:06:10.773
- [Kelly] You do?

00:06:10.773 --> 00:06:11.606
- But, but you know,

00:06:11.606 --> 00:06:12.439
the funny thing is-
- Can you teach that?

00:06:12.439 --> 00:06:14.940
- Well, we all lie a little
bit, don't we? I mean-

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:16.980
- One hundred percent. I lie
to my children all the time.

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:18.066
I love you. But yeah.

00:06:18.066 --> 00:06:18.899
- I mean, it's bad-

00:06:18.899 --> 00:06:20.909
- I tell them things
are closed all the time.

00:06:20.909 --> 00:06:22.522
- It's bad business to lie-

00:06:22.522 --> 00:06:23.850
- Totally.
- But we all do.

00:06:23.850 --> 00:06:27.126
And usually over the most
mundane, like ridiculous things.

00:06:27.126 --> 00:06:27.959
There's always-
- I don't wanna go

00:06:27.959 --> 00:06:29.707
to Chuck E. Cheese right now. Yeah.

00:06:29.707 --> 00:06:30.900
- "Honey, I'm five minutes away,"

00:06:30.900 --> 00:06:32.779
when you haven't left the,
whatever the location is.

00:06:32.779 --> 00:06:33.612
- One hundred percent.

00:06:33.612 --> 00:06:34.950
- That's me. Yeah.
- Yeah.

00:06:34.950 --> 00:06:36.450
- What's cool about this particular show

00:06:36.450 --> 00:06:39.450
is it's a 10 part limited series.

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:42.480
And other than the pilot
and the final episode,

00:06:42.480 --> 00:06:46.560
is you can, you can watch
out of sequence episode four,

00:06:46.560 --> 00:06:49.320
go to episode seven, go
back to episode three,

00:06:49.320 --> 00:06:51.592
because each one hour of the episode

00:06:51.592 --> 00:06:54.390
is its own kind of standalone movie.

00:06:54.390 --> 00:06:57.226
Charlie Cale, Natasha's
character, solving a murder.

00:06:57.226 --> 00:06:59.730
And because it's Ryan
Johnson, who's, you know,

00:06:59.730 --> 00:07:00.940
out there right now with "Glass Onion."

00:07:00.940 --> 00:07:01.830
- [Kelly] Yeah, yeah.

00:07:01.830 --> 00:07:04.920
- You know, it's gonna be,
it'll have shocking violence,

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:06.750
it'll have a lot of humor,

00:07:06.750 --> 00:07:09.270
and it'll have, you know, you
guessing all along the way

00:07:09.270 --> 00:07:10.860
about how the whole
thing's gonna be solved.

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:11.693
- I love that.

00:07:11.693 --> 00:07:12.540
Great storytelling.

00:07:12.540 --> 00:07:13.380
- Amazing storytelling.

00:07:13.380 --> 00:07:14.940
- That must be fun to
be on a set like that.

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:17.090
- And Natasha, have you ever
had Natasha on as a guest?

00:07:17.090 --> 00:07:18.870
- No, I love her.

00:07:18.870 --> 00:07:20.490
- She's a writer and director as well,

00:07:20.490 --> 00:07:22.440
and she actually writes and
directs one of the episodes-

00:07:22.440 --> 00:07:23.940
- Brilliant.
- Throughout the season.

00:07:23.940 --> 00:07:25.200
But it's her personality.

00:07:25.200 --> 00:07:27.240
It's her energy. What she
brings to the character,

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:28.260
it makes it totally unique.

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:29.249
I mean, the architecture of the show,

00:07:29.249 --> 00:07:32.340
it's a genre we're all very familiar with

00:07:32.340 --> 00:07:33.930
and it's very, very popular, you know-

00:07:33.930 --> 00:07:35.399
- But it's cool to have
a different spin on it.

00:07:35.399 --> 00:07:38.089
- It's her nature that
really makes it compelling.

00:07:38.089 --> 00:07:40.350
- Yeah. I have to ask you this,

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:41.587
because this is the craziest
thing I've ever heard.

00:07:41.587 --> 00:07:44.280
Literally, and I've been
doing this four seasons.

00:07:44.280 --> 00:07:45.930
Is it true that your mom

00:07:45.930 --> 00:07:48.870
had you live on Alcatraz a
little bit when you're a child?

00:07:48.870 --> 00:07:50.100
Like what's that story?

00:07:50.100 --> 00:07:53.190
- Yeah. You know, I was born
and raised in San Francisco.

00:07:53.190 --> 00:07:54.900
And briefly in 1969,

00:07:54.900 --> 00:07:57.360
a group of young Native American students

00:07:57.360 --> 00:07:58.950
from San Francisco State decided

00:07:58.950 --> 00:08:00.930
to take over Alcatraz Island

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:02.580
as kind of a political statement.

00:08:02.580 --> 00:08:03.660
As most of you know,

00:08:03.660 --> 00:08:06.737
Alcatraz was a federal
penitentiary up until 1962,

00:08:06.737 --> 00:08:10.950
and it remained out of
operation until 1969.

00:08:10.950 --> 00:08:12.600
And these young students were smart enough

00:08:12.600 --> 00:08:13.631
to cite an old treaty

00:08:13.631 --> 00:08:18.090
from the 1800s that cited
any government held land

00:08:18.090 --> 00:08:21.510
that was not being used
reverts back to the sovereignty

00:08:21.510 --> 00:08:22.800
of native people.

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:25.330
So it was a political gesture

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:29.730
that was national news,
international news for that matter,

00:08:29.730 --> 00:08:31.890
and my mom was a part of
the group that then settled

00:08:31.890 --> 00:08:34.320
on the island during
the 18 month occupation.

00:08:34.320 --> 00:08:37.260
I was five years old
and I lived in the city,

00:08:37.260 --> 00:08:38.983
but I would say every weekend,

00:08:38.983 --> 00:08:42.450
at least two nights a week,
we were living on the island.

00:08:42.450 --> 00:08:44.321
- Your mom is so cool.

00:08:44.321 --> 00:08:46.020
- That's my brother Peter,

00:08:46.020 --> 00:08:48.363
who's a filmmaker now, and
my youngest sister, Verushka.

00:08:48.363 --> 00:08:51.067
I was actually meant to be in
that shot, but I got bored.

00:08:51.067 --> 00:08:52.440
(everyone laughing)

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:53.273
- You got bored on Alcatraz?

00:08:53.273 --> 00:08:54.450
- I was running around the other part

00:08:54.450 --> 00:08:55.283
of the cell blocks there.

00:08:55.283 --> 00:08:56.341
- Yeah. I've been to Alcatraz.

00:08:56.341 --> 00:08:58.557
That would be a scary place
kind of for a kid, or fun.

00:08:58.557 --> 00:09:01.470
- But it wasn't scary at
all because, you know,

00:09:01.470 --> 00:09:03.660
I think parents are different
about their responsibilities

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:04.770
and raising kids these days.

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:06.390
It wasn't that my mom
didn't care about us.

00:09:06.390 --> 00:09:07.350
She was like, "Just go play."

00:09:07.350 --> 00:09:08.183
And so we'd go-
- Free. Yeah.

00:09:08.183 --> 00:09:10.017
- We'd go onto the
industrial side of the island

00:09:10.017 --> 00:09:13.320
and you know, throw rocks
at windows and go crabbing.

00:09:13.320 --> 00:09:14.957
You know, we'd go down on the shoreline

00:09:14.957 --> 00:09:17.700
and look for crabs, et cetera.

00:09:17.700 --> 00:09:19.830
But it was the ultimate
playground, really,

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:22.140
for me and my brother
and my three sisters.

00:09:22.140 --> 00:09:23.760
- Yeah. It's just a really cool thing too,

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:27.570
to introduce something that's important

00:09:27.570 --> 00:09:28.985
and show your kids to
think outside of yourself

00:09:28.985 --> 00:09:30.720
at such young ages. That's cool.

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:32.527
- Yeah. You know, this
was in the aftermath

00:09:32.527 --> 00:09:35.610
of my mom getting a
divorce from my father.

00:09:35.610 --> 00:09:36.510
And she was an immigrant.

00:09:36.510 --> 00:09:39.720
She came from Lima, Peru at the age of 14.

00:09:39.720 --> 00:09:43.140
And so I think she felt
a little left untethered

00:09:43.140 --> 00:09:46.020
and so when she found
this native community

00:09:46.020 --> 00:09:48.180
and it embraced her fully,

00:09:48.180 --> 00:09:50.610
she found, in a way, her
own sense of identity.

00:09:50.610 --> 00:09:52.470
And it's an identity, a native identity

00:09:52.470 --> 00:09:55.350
that she left us as a legacy.

00:09:55.350 --> 00:09:58.500
You know, she actually called
the young Mohawk leader,

00:09:58.500 --> 00:10:00.930
Richard Oakes, who was the
leader of the young student group

00:10:00.930 --> 00:10:01.860
that took over the island,

00:10:01.860 --> 00:10:03.210
and got him on the phone when he was

00:10:03.210 --> 00:10:05.391
on the local (indistinct) PBS channel.

00:10:05.391 --> 00:10:08.070
And she said, "I'm Native
American from South America.

00:10:08.070 --> 00:10:09.090
Does that count?"

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:11.520
And he said, "Indians of
all tribes come down."

00:10:11.520 --> 00:10:12.359
And she said it's the first time

00:10:12.359 --> 00:10:16.050
that she was in the country
that she felt like she belonged.

00:10:16.050 --> 00:10:18.924
But what's unusual is this
habit we have had historically

00:10:18.924 --> 00:10:21.930
of trying to put everyone
in a specific box.

00:10:21.930 --> 00:10:24.030
This is what you are,
and that's your identity.

00:10:24.030 --> 00:10:26.790
Leave the identifying to the individual.

00:10:26.790 --> 00:10:29.430
It's something I continue to
hand down to my own children.

00:10:29.430 --> 00:10:30.784
- That's cool. You had a rad mama.

00:10:30.784 --> 00:10:33.005
(audience applauding)

00:10:33.005 --> 00:10:35.933
- And listen, she's still doing the do.

00:10:35.933 --> 00:10:39.420
I mean, my mother is, she's
the center of the universe

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:40.320
for all of us.

00:10:40.320 --> 00:10:42.840
- That's so cool. Not everybody
gets that kind of family.

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:44.367
That's kind of magical.
- That's true.

00:10:44.367 --> 00:10:45.960
And that's the American story.

00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:48.510
I mean, an immigrant
comes to this country.

00:10:48.510 --> 00:10:49.467
She has children.
- Absolutely.

00:10:49.467 --> 00:10:51.767
- And she ends up finding
a way to contribute.

